 "notes": "Theory paper (Arias, Cadamuro, Goodsell, Jaeckel, Redondo, Ringwald 2012) on the misalignment mechanism for WISP cold dark matter. It derives its own new cosmological/observational exclusions on hidden-photon (dark-photon) dark matter, which is the main novel result. Reported curve = upper boundary of the pink Allowed HP CDM region in Fig. 5 (m_gamma prime [eV] vs kinetic mixing chi), extracted from the vector figure HP.pdf in the arXiv e-print (fill path with color red drawn semi-transparent, hence pink). Above this curve, the hidden photon cannot constitute all of the cold dark matter. The boundary is a composite: at low mass it is the paper-derived tau2>1 (resonant depletion) bound at chi~1e-9; the sharp drop and rise (m ~ 1e-9 to 1e-2 eV) are the paper-derived CMB-distortions and N_eff bounds, peaking at chi~1.2e-8 near m~1e-2 eV; at high mass (m ~ 1e5-1e6 eV, near 2 m_e) it is the paper-reproduced X-rays bound from gamma prime -> 3 gamma decay. In the intermediate mass range (m ~ 1 - 1e4 eV) the visible boundary tracks the pre-existing astrophysical Solar-Lifetime/HB regions redrawn from other refs, not this paper. Axis calibration from tick/label positions: x 18.457 px/decade (-10 at 89.15 px), y 22.39 px/decade (-16 at 257.6 px). Limit assumes the HP is 100 percent of the cosmological DM (rho_CDM = 1.17 keV/cm^3, Eq. 2.11); it is not a local-halo direct-detection bound, so dm_density_assumed is null. No formal statistical confidence level stated (order-of-magnitude cosmological bounds). The paper also presents an ALP g_agamma plot (Fig. 1) and future experimental sensitivity forecasts, which are not reported here.",
